  <!-- Trinkets, wave one. #603.

       Vanilla's category is six objects, and `Trinkets 1` through `Trinkets 8` offer the same six
       at every tier. That sameness is correct and is not being changed here - a folding chair is a
       folding chair, and the decision not to tier-grade trinkets is recorded in #603. The gap is
       breadth, so these six go into all eight tables.

       They are entered at **weight 11 against vanilla's 35**, which is not a judgement about how
       interesting they are. STYLEGUIDE.md 3.2.1 caps this fork's share of a vanilla loot table at
       half and says so explicitly: where completeness tips a table past half, the fix is a lower
       per-item weight, never less content. Eighteen fork trinkets at 11 come to 198 against
       vanilla's 210. So vanilla's six stay the ones a player meets most, which is the texture that
       ceiling exists to protect.

       The register, read off the existing five (PlasticTree is furniture by weight, value and
       unknown appearance, so it is not part of the reference set): each does exactly ONE small,
       ordinary, human thing. Not a stat, not a mechanic - an affordance. Sitting. Drawing.
       Grinding salt. The failure modes are gag items and trinkets that turn out to be secretly
       useful, and both are worse than leaving the category thin.

       Inherited from `Trinket` and deliberately not overridden:
         - `TinkerItem Bits="0" CanDisassemble="true"`. Scrapping is half the category's stated
           purpose. `BoxOfCrayons` is the one vanilla trinket that opts out, and these follow the
           five that do not.
         - `<tag Name="DynamicObjectsTable:Trinkets" />`. The four pocket-sized vanilla trinkets
           keep it; `FoldingChair` and `PlasticTree` `*delete` it because they are furniture.
           These are pocket-sized, so they keep it.
         - `Examiner Unknown="UnknownOddTrinket" Complexity="1"`. Unidentified appearances are a
           shared pool of 49 blueprints, not per-item art, so this costs nothing per trinket.

       Values sit at 5, with the hourglass at 10 as the one worked-glass-and-frame object - the
       same shape as vanilla pricing five at 5 and `BoxOfCrayons` at 10. `<stag Name="Trinket" />`
       is what earns the value-curve exemption in STYLEGUIDE.md §3.2, and tools/validate_mod.py
       reads that marker, so it is load-bearing rather than decorative.

       Tiles are 16x24 three-colour PNGs: black is the foreground and takes ColorString, white is
       the DetailColor, transparent is the background. That mapping is the wiki's, not a guess -
       it is the reverse of what the file bytes suggest at a glance. -->

